AT(1)                          P32W Unix                           AT(1)

NAME
       at, atq, atrm - run commands once, later

SYNOPSIS
       at hh:mm [mm/dd | yyyy/mm/dd]
       at now [+ n minutes | hours | days]
       atq
       atrm job ...

DESCRIPTION
       at reads shell commands from the standard input, up to end
       of file (^D at a terminal), and queues them to run once, at
       the time given, as you, in the current directory. It prints
       the job's name and when it will run.

       hh:mm is a 24-hour local time (see tz(5)), the hour one or
       two digits and the minute two: today, or tomorrow if that
       minute has already passed. A date mm/dd (this year) or
       yyyy/mm/dd may follow; it must exist (no 13/40 or 2/30; the
       year 1970 to 2037), and a date and time that have passed are
       refused. now + n units counts from the present; the unit is
       min, mins, minute, minutes, hour, hours, day or days, and
       the +, the number (0 to 9999) and the unit must be separate
       words. Times are rounded down to the minute.

       The job is a file in /usr/spool/at, mode 0600, owned by you
       and named by the time in seconds since 1970 (with .1, .2 ...
       if that name is taken). Its first lines record what the job
       will need, as comments to sh:

              # at uid gid directory
              # env NAME=value
              # umask 022

       one env line for each of HOME, USER, LOGNAME, PATH, SHELL
       and TZ that is set; the commands follow.

       cron(8) looks at the queue once a minute and starts every
       job whose time has come, with sh(1), as the user who owns
       the job's file, in the recorded directory, with the saved
       environment and umask. The standard input is /dev/null; the
       standard output and error are appended to at.out in the
       user's home directory (created with mode 0600). If cron is
       not running, jobs wait.

       atq lists the jobs waiting, in time order: the name, the
       time and the owner; root sees everyone's, others only their
       own.

       atrm removes the named jobs (names as atq shows them). Users
       may remove only their own; root may remove any.

EXAMPLES
       $ at 23:30
       tar cf /mnt/ken.tar /usr/ken
       ^D
       job 1792020600 at Wed Oct 14 23:30:00 2026
       $ echo make | at now + 2 hours
       $ at 9:00 2026/12/24 < reminder
       $ atq
       1792020600     Wed Oct 14 23:30:00 2026  ken
       $ atrm 1792020600

FILES
       /usr/spool/at          the queue
       $HOME/at.out           the jobs' output
       /tmp/atNNN             a running job's copy of its file,
                              removed when the job ends

EXIT STATUS
       at: 0 when queued; 1 if the job file cannot be made; 2 for a
       time it does not understand (it prints the usage), a date
       that does not exist ("at: 2/30: bad date") or a time that
       has passed ("that time has passed"). atq: 0; 2 if given an
       argument. atrm: 0; 1 if a job does not exist (atrm: job: no
       such job) or is not yours (not yours); 2 with no job named.

SEE ALSO
       cron(8), crontab(5), sh(1), date(1), sleep(1)

BUGS
       There is no -f file, no -l or -d, no noon or midnight, no
       month names, and no batch. Variables other than the six
       above are not saved. cron starts at most 32 jobs a minute.
       A job that cannot change to its directory is not run (the
       reason is written to at.out).

       The queue directory is writable by everyone, so a user can
       delete (but not change) another user's job with rm.
